The problem statement involves an undirected graph G of n nodes (ranging from 1 to 1000) with edges of the form (u, v), representing a direct edge from node u to node v, with (u, v) != (v, u). This means that the edges are bi-directional, forming connected nodes. The task is to check if G contains a cycle, and to return True if a cycle is found, and False otherwise.

It is given that the edges can form multiple cycles in the graph, and they can be formed using options like loops (of cycles), and combinations of adjacent node pairs. Loops form a cycle when all edges are of the form (u, u), leading to adjoining nodes themselves forming a circle. When two adjacent node pairs, u and v (with u != v), form a cycle, the combination 'u - v' forms the cycle, and this can happen in any of the combinations u ---> v, v ---> u, or u ---> v onwards, forming a loop.

These conditions imply different possibilities for the cycle formation. Assume the given edges are arranged linearly; if edges exist from node u to exact node v, but there is no edge from v to u, node v ----- v (linear expansion loop from node v) becomes the cycle. If they exist from u to v, and option v exists, then v ------ u (linear expansion loop from node v) becomes the cycle. If both u and v are among the node pair, but again only options for v or u lead to v ... u (cycle from u represented as 10, 9, 8, 7 ... 1 loop or with negative index of n if a negative edge(u, v) exists), then this forms a cycle as well.

